A||||8||RobertVol||Body Panel Removal||||5||
Z||000000||RobertVol||07-29-2001||10:11 PM||||I am thinking of getting my car painted. Of course I want to save money so I will take it to maaco. I was wondering..would not be easier to remove the panels form the frame and take to the shop and have them painted seperately? IF so How hard is to remove them? <P>If not then I guess just removing the Black strip molding would help in the overspray. <P>Suggestions?  <P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P><A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RobsGT2.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||DecadenceR||07-30-2001||12:28 AM||||I dunno about anyone elses opinion here. but as for me, I just did my rear decklid, fascia, passenger door panel, and driver side fender. And there's NO WAY I would take off all my panels to take them and get painted, and then have to put them back on again. The few I did was pain ehough for me.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||07-30-2001||01:00 AM||||I had thought about the same thing, Robert. Of course, I am changing color and completely redoing the car. Other than that, I wouldn't even consider it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||6
Z||000003||Phil||07-30-2001||06:10 AM||||I would check with Macco- they might charge you more to do the separate pieces.- more handling and takes up more room  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||07-30-2001||06:10 AM||||I would check with Macco- they might charge you more to do the separate pieces.- more handling and takes up more shop room  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||07-30-2001||05:19 PM||||I dont think Maaco will do it all apart. Ive only done a few cars that were apart. They were total restorations jobs that were chemical stripped. Both were reassembled by the owner. I wont do one all apart. We changed color on Lisas GT and didnt take anything but taillights, headlites, and a few moldings off. If its masked careful a color change is no big deal, especially on Fiero where door jams, underhood and trunk are black anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||07-30-2001||06:36 PM||||The Fiero was originally painted with the panels off.  If you want all the edges perfect and now overspray anywhere then its the only way.  It is definitely not easier but not that hard either.  I would not argue with either method both have their pluses and minuses. Mine had all panels removed except the fastback and windshild piece. There were 43 seperate pieces to paint. The results were great. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7080039-2_6.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||07-31-2001||03:56 PM||||Skitime,<P>That's an interesting engine cover.  Do you have close up pictures that you could post?  Details?<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||07-31-2001||10:15 PM||||You done a great job skitime. but personally it wouldnt bother me at all if a diff color showed up when qtr panel gets ripped off in a broadside, Dont need to do what no one will ever see. I dont wash and wax the bottom of my car either, but I have seen it done many times. The few I have painted apart was because they were metal bodied and they wanted inside of seams painted to keep them from corroding.......Fiero is plastic so that would be a mute point.||||reg||1
